================================================================================================================================

# 1. PURPOSE

================================================================================================================================

This document is the authoritative master reference for:

**Atomic Delete Persistence**

within the Elite X Manual Trade system.

The purpose of this feature was to move Manual Trade deletion from an uncontrolled client-side destructive operation into a controlled server-side persistence boundary.

The architecture must guarantee:

```text
Authenticated User
        ↓
Server-side Delete Request
        ↓
Validated Delete Target
        ↓
Atomic Database Delete
```

The operation must modify canonical executions only.

================================================================================================================================

# 2. CORE ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLE

================================================================================================================================

Elite X is execution-first.

```text
Executions
    ↓
pairTrades()
    ↓
Derived Trades
```

Therefore Manual Delete does not directly delete reconstructed trades.

It deletes the underlying execution state.

After deletion:

```text
Remaining Executions
        ↓
pairTrades()
        ↓
New Derived State
```

This keeps the execution ledger as the source of truth.

================================================================================================================================

# 3. ORIGINAL DELETE PROBLEM

================================================================================================================================

Manual Trade deletion previously relied on direct client-side database deletion.

That approach created several concerns:

```text
Client-controlled destructive operation
        ↓
Ownership boundary
        ↓
Target validation
        ↓
Lifecycle targeting
        ↓
Partial Exit targeting
```

The delete operation therefore needed a dedicated server-side boundary.

================================================================================================================================

# 4. NEW ATOMIC DELETE ARCHITECTURE

================================================================================================================================

The current architecture is:

```text
Manual Trade UI
        ↓
POST /api/trades/delete
        ↓
Authenticate User
        ↓
deleteManualExecutionsAtomically()
        ↓
Supabase RPC
        ↓
Delete canonical execution(s)
        ↓
pairTrades()
        ↓
Derived state
```

The client no longer performs the destructive database operation directly.

================================================================================================================================

# 5. MANUAL DELETE API

================================================================================================================================

Current file:

```text
app/api/trades/delete/route.ts
```

The API:

1. reads the Authorization header;
2. extracts the Bearer token;
3. authenticates the user through Supabase;
4. obtains the authenticated `user.id`;
5. validates the requested delete mode;
6. calls the server-side atomic delete helper;
7. returns success only when deletion succeeds;
8. returns an error when authentication or deletion fails.

The client does not provide the authoritative ownership identity.

================================================================================================================================

# 6. SERVER-SIDE DELETE HELPER

================================================================================================================================

Current file:

```text
lib/server/trades/deleteManualExecutionsAtomically.ts
```

The helper supports:

```ts
type ManualDeleteMode =
  | "execution_id"
  | "contract_key";
```

The helper validates the required information for the selected mode and calls:

```text
delete_manual_executions_atomically
```

through the server-side Supabase admin client.

================================================================================================================================

# 7. DELETE MODES

================================================================================================================================

There are two distinct deletion scopes.

### MODE 1 — EXECUTION ID

```text
deleteMode = "execution_id"
```

Used for:

```text
Partial Exit deletion
```

This targets the exact execution selected by the user.

### MODE 2 — CONTRACT KEY

```text
deleteMode = "contract_key"
```

Used when the selected Manual Trade represents the complete lifecycle.

This removes the Manual lifecycle represented by that contract key.

================================================================================================================================

# 8. PARTIAL EXIT DELETE RULE

================================================================================================================================

This is one of the most important rules in the system.

For Partial Exit deletion:

```text
DELETE EXACT EXECUTION ID
```

Never delete the entire lifecycle.

Example:

```text
BUY 100

SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20  ← selected exit
SELL 20
```

The selected exit is removed.

The remaining executions become:

```text
BUY 100

SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
```

The system then reconstructs:

```text
20 OPEN
```

No manual restoration is performed.

================================================================================================================================

# 9. COMPLETE LIFECYCLE DELETE RULE

================================================================================================================================

When the selected Manual Trade represents the complete lifecycle:

```text
deleteMode = "contract_key"
```

The database deletes all Manual executions matching that lifecycle key for the authenticated user.

Example:

```text
BUY 100
SELL 100
```

Both executions belong to:

```text
MANUAL-...
```

The lifecycle is removed from the canonical execution ledger.

The derived trade then disappears naturally after reconstruction.

================================================================================================================================

# 10. MANUAL-ONLY PROTECTION

================================================================================================================================

Manual executions are identified through:

```text
contractKey starts with:

MANUAL-
```

The database RPC enforces this requirement.

For execution-level deletion:

```text
contract_key LIKE 'MANUAL-%'
```

must match.

For lifecycle deletion:

```text
p_contract_key LIKE 'MANUAL-%'
```

must be true.

This prevents the supported Manual Delete path from intentionally targeting broker executions.

================================================================================================================================

# 11. USER OWNERSHIP PROTECTION

================================================================================================================================

The API authenticates the user.

The server obtains:

```text
user.id
```

The helper passes that ID to the database RPC.

The RPC scopes the deletion by:

```text
user_id = authenticated user
```

Therefore the supported delete path does not trust a client-provided arbitrary user ID.

================================================================================================================================

# 12. DATABASE RPC

================================================================================================================================

Current database function:

```text
public.delete_manual_executions_atomically(
    uuid,
    text,
    text,
    text
)
returns integer
```

The function is configured as:

```text
SECURITY DEFINER
set search_path = public
```

It validates the delete mode and target before performing the deletion.

================================================================================================================================

# 13. EXECUTION-LEVEL DELETE

================================================================================================================================

For:

```text
p_delete_mode = 'execution_id'
```

the RPC deletes only an execution satisfying:

```text
id = requested execution ID

AND

user_id = requested user

AND

contract_key LIKE 'MANUAL-%'
```

This provides three important boundaries:

```text
Exact execution
+
Correct user
+
Manual lifecycle
```

================================================================================================================================

# 14. CONTRACT-KEY DELETE

================================================================================================================================

For:

```text
p_delete_mode = 'contract_key'
```

the RPC first requires the contract key to identify a Manual lifecycle.

It then deletes:

```text
contract_key = requested contract key

AND

user_id = requested user
```

This makes lifecycle deletion explicit rather than allowing arbitrary execution deletion.

================================================================================================================================

# 15. ZERO-ROW PROTECTION

================================================================================================================================

A delete request that matches no Manual execution is not silently treated as successful.

The RPC obtains the PostgreSQL deleted-row count.

If:

```text
deleted_count = 0
```

it raises:

```text
No matching manual execution was found.
```

This is important because:

```text
No rows deleted
```

is not equivalent to:

```text
Delete successful
```

================================================================================================================================

# 16. TRANSACTIONAL BEHAVIOR

================================================================================================================================

The delete function runs within PostgreSQL's transactional execution context.

The intended sequence is:

```text
Validate
    ↓
DELETE
    ↓
Check deleted count
    ↓
Return success
```

If an exception occurs:

```text
DELETE
    ↓
ERROR
    ↓
ROLLBACK
```

The database does not intentionally leave a partially completed delete operation.

================================================================================================================================

# 17. WHY THERE IS NO DELETE + INSERT HERE

================================================================================================================================

Atomic Edit requires:

```text
DELETE + INSERT
```

because an edit replaces old executions with corrected executions.

Delete is different.

Delete only requires:

```text
DELETE
```

Therefore there is no unnecessary delete/reinsert sequence for Manual Delete.

The atomic safety requirement here is focused on:

```text
Server-side authorization
+
target validation
+
manual-only protection
+
transactional execution
+
zero-row failure
```

================================================================================================================================

# 18. FIFO / RECONSTRUCTION AFTER DELETE

================================================================================================================================

Authoritative reconstruction:

```text
lib/parsers/pairTrades.ts
```

Manual Delete does not modify this file.

After execution deletion:

```text
Remaining executions
        ↓
pairTrades()
        ↓
Reconstructed positions/trades
```

The resulting state is therefore derived automatically.

================================================================================================================================

# 19. PARTIAL EXIT DELETION DOES NOT RESTORE QUANTITY

================================================================================================================================

Never add special logic such as:

```text
if exit deleted:
    restore quantity
```

That would create a second source of truth.

Correct behavior:

```text
Delete execution
        ↓
Remaining executions
        ↓
pairTrades()
        ↓
Open quantity naturally derived
```

Example:

Before:

```text
BUY 100
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
```

Result:

```text
0 OPEN
```

After deleting one exit:

```text
BUY 100
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
SELL 20
```

Result:

```text
20 OPEN
```

No explicit restoration code is required.

================================================================================================================================

# 20. DELETE OF COMPLETE LIFECYCLE

================================================================================================================================

Example:

```text
BUY 100
SELL 100
```

Delete lifecycle:

```text
contractKey = MANUAL-...
```

The canonical executions disappear.

The derived trade disappears because its source executions no longer exist.

There is no separate:

```text
DELETE reconstructed trade
```

operation.

================================================================================================================================

# 21. CLIENT SUCCESS BEHAVIOR

================================================================================================================================

The client waits for the API response.

Conceptually:

```text
POST /api/trades/delete
        ↓
SUCCESS
        ↓
close/reload
```

If the server rejects the request:

```text
API ERROR
        ↓
Delete is not considered successful
```

The UI must not falsely represent a failed delete as completed.

================================================================================================================================

# 22. DATABASE SECURITY

================================================================================================================================

The RPC is configured with:

```text
SECURITY DEFINER
set search_path = public
```

Execution privileges are restricted.

The intended permission model is:

```text
public
    → EXECUTE revoked

service_role
    → EXECUTE granted
```

The client therefore does not directly invoke the privileged database operation.

